  means adding a second endpoint later is one line instead of three. This isn't speculative generality; it's not
  duplicating the host three times.

  [1m[38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m is a separate pure function.[0m It takes a hostname string and returns an env key. That's what makes
  the mapping testable without a browser, and it's the only piece with real logic in it.

  [1mUnknown hostname falls back to production.[0m A config module that throws takes the entire page down at load. Falling
  back to production means an unrecognized host still gets a working app pointed at the real API. The tradeoff: a typo'd
  staging hostname silently talks to production instead of failing loudly. I think that's the right call for a login
  form, but say so if you'd rather it be loud.

  [1mChanges to existing files:[0m
  - [38;5;153mapp.js[39m — drop [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m, add [38;5;153mimport { settings } from "./settings.js"[39m, reference [38;5;153msettings.apiBaseUrl[39m in [38;5;153mlogin()[39m.
  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — [38;5;153m<script type="module" src="app.js"></script>[39m.

  I'll need the real staging and local hostnames/URLs from you; the values above are placeholders.

  [1mSection 2: Tooling[0m

  This project has no test runner, no linter, and no dependencies. [38;5;153mresolveEnvironment[39m is the first piece of genuinely
  testable logic here — a pure string-to-string function with branch cases. Setting up tooling is cheapest now, before
  there's code to retrofit, but it's also a real scope increase over "move a constant."

